<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Raw minerals a settlement digs out of the ground (system: mining). Its own file rather than more entries
    in Items_Resource.xml — DefLoader.AddDirectory loads every XML file under a Def folder recursively, so
    additive content needs no edit to a shared one (CLAUDE.md). The vein each of these comes out of is in
    ThingDefs_Buildings/Buildings_Mineable.xml.

    WHY THESE, AND WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM
    Before this module exactly one ThingDef in all of content was mineable (MineableSteel) and it dropped
    nothing, because nothing read mineableThing. One rock and one resource is not a resource economy, and
    this project's whole arc is sticks and stones to exotic technology, so the set below is chosen to give
    every era on that ladder something in the ground worth digging for:

      flint      sticks and stones   (research: StoneTools; DepositDefOf.Flint already exists)
      salt       agrarian/classical  (preservation and trade; DepositDefOf.Salt already exists)
      copper/tin bronze              (research: Copper, Bronze, BronzeWeapons already exist in the tree)
      steel      medieval            (already shipped, see Buildings_Natural.xml)
      silver     classical onward    (already shipped as the trade currency, Items_Currency.xml)
      gold/jade  classical onward    (prestige goods rather than tools)
      coal       industrial          (already shipped; SiteWeights.xml already ramps Coal's worth by era)
      uranium    information/exotic
      plasteel   exotic

    Gold, jade, silver, uranium and plasteel are RimWorld's own mineables, ported by name. Flint, salt,
    copper and tin are NOT in RimWorld: RimWorld starts at a tech level this game spends several eras
    reaching, so its ore table has no bronze age in it. Those four are this port's own additions and are
    recorded as such here (CLAUDE.md: translations are recorded, not improvised).

    MarketValue: RimWorld's own item values were not available to check against here, so none of the numbers
    below is sourced. What is deliberate is the ORDERING — flint < salt < tin < copper < steel < gold < jade
    < uranium < plasteel — hung on the one anchor shipped content already fixed, Steel at 1.9. The tests
    assert that chain and nothing else about these numbers.

    Two shipped minerals sit outside the chain on purpose and are not asserted against it. Silver is the
    trade currency and is 1 by definition (Items_Currency.xml explains why), so it is priced by what it is
    for rather than by where it sits on a ladder. Coal's 1.0 is the Economy module's number: SiteWeights.xml
    already ramps what a coal deposit is worth from nothing through Classical to a great deal in Industrial,
    which is era-dependent in a way a single MarketValue cannot be.

    None of these carries stuffProps. They are commodities, not building materials — the same call
    Items_Resource.xml already made for Coal, and made deliberately: adding new Metallic stuffs would change
    what every stuff-picking caller in the game rolls.
  -->

  <!--
    statBases is deliberately NOT on this parent: every child declares its own complete block, so a reader
    sees one def's stats in one place and no child has to reason about merging with an inherited list.
  -->
  <ThingDef Name="BaseMineral" Abstract="True">
    <category>Item</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Things.Thing</thingClass>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Flint</defName>
    <label>flint</label>
    <description>Hard nodules that fracture to a cutting edge. The first thing anyone ever mined, and still the sharpest thing a people without metal can hold.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>0.4</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Salt</defName>
    <label>salt</label>
    <description>Rock salt cut from a buried seam. Keeps meat through a winter, which is why people who have it trade it and people who do not walk a long way for it.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>0.8</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Tin</defName>
    <label>tin</label>
    <description>A soft, low-melting metal, scarce almost everywhere. Worth little alone and everything beside copper: the two together make bronze.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li><li>Metals</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>1.4</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Copper</defName>
    <label>copper</label>
    <description>The first metal worked at scale — soft enough to hammer cold, common enough to find, and the half of bronze that a settlement can usually dig up itself.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li><li>Metals</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>1.6</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Gold</defName>
    <label>gold</label>
    <description>Heavy, soft and untarnishing. Useless for a tool and unmatched for saying who you are.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li><li>Metals</li></thingCategories>
    <stackLimit>500</stackLimit>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>10</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Jade</defName>
    <label>jade</label>
    <description>A dense green stone that takes a deep polish and blunts the tools that shape it. Carved into ornaments by peoples who had nothing harder to carve it with.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>12</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Uranium</defName>
    <label>uranium</label>
    <description>Dense, faintly warm ore. Worth nothing at all until a civilization knows what it is, and then worth almost anything.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li><li>Metals</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>18</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Plasteel</defName>
    <label>plasteel</label>
    <description>A metal-ceramic composite far beyond anything a forge can make, found compacted in old rock. Whoever left it there is not around to ask.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li><li>Metals</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>25</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
